  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A frontal system nearby will maintain rain\nchances through Sunday with below average temperatures.\n\nThe latest surface analysis places a cold front across North\nCarolina which stretches southwestward to the Lower Mississippi\nValley. As stout high pressure sits across the Great Lakes, a\ncold air damming (CAD) wedge signature is evident across the \nlocal area. In response, mainly northerly flow is occurring \nwhich coupled with ample cloud cover is keeping temperatures in \nthe mid 50s to mid 60s. The warmer side of this range is \noccurring closer to the Mason-Dixon Line where a few cloud \nbreaks are evident on visible satellite imagery. Looking on \nradar, light rain continues to overrun the surface front. \nDespite the anomalous moisture in place, the stable nature of \nthe air mass coupled with some low-level dry air has limited \nrainfall amounts. Hourly totals are not amounting to much more \nthan a trace to 0.05 inches. For those south of I-66, such \nactivity will likely impact these areas through the rest of the \nafternoon and into the early evening.\n\nFor tonight, a thick stratus deck remains overhead which will\nhold overnight lows in the 50s. Some patchy fog is possible,\nparticularly in the more rural low-lying areas. Rain chances do\nremain just before daybreak Sunday as the next impulse traverses\nthe quasi-stationary front. A band of light rain, perhaps\nlocally moderate in nature, will track northeastward across the\nregion through at least the first half of Sunday. A persistent\nnortheasterly wind should again hold temperatures down in the\n60s. Any rain chances should largely come to an end just before\nsundown Sunday. Overnight conditions should be fairly similar to\ntonight with lows again in the 50s.\n\nAs a deep upper trough tracks across far eastern Canada, a more\npotent cold front sweeps through the northeastern U.S. on\nMonday. This will favor increasing sunshine, but also a large\nuptick in northwesterly winds. Afternoon gusts up to 20 to 25\nmph are possible, but with highs returning to the upper 60s to\nmid 70s (upper 50s to 60s in the mountains).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freezing temperatures are possible to\nthe west of the Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nA strong area of high pressure will build over the area next \nweek, leading to an extended period of dry conditions. As a cooler \nairmass works in and high pressure builds overhead, frost and \nfreezing temperatures may be possible to the west of the Blue Ridge \nboth Monday night and Tuesday night. We'll start to see slightly \nwarmer air build in from the south and west later in the week. High \ntemperatures at lower elevations are forecast to be in the 60s \n(upper 50s/low 60s mountains) on Tuesday, upper 60s to low 70s on \nWednesday (60s mountains), and mid 70s (mid-upper 60s mountains) on \nWednesday and Thursday. Low temperatures will gradually moderate \nfrom the 30s/40s early in the week, to the upper 40s/low 50s later \nin the week.",
